StFttDbMaker/macro/FttDataWindows -- FTT (sTGC) per-VMM time calibration
========================================================================

The table is Calibrations/ftt/fttDataWindowsB: for each of the 384 VMMs, a time
window (min,max) and a dbcid ANCHOR, the reference tick that window is measured
from.

What is here
------------
  fttDataWindow.C        produces a calibration from MuDst, one run at a time
  fttDataWindow_db.C     pushes a calibration into the DB, and reads it back
  data/                  the calibrations we have measured (see below).
                         NOT IN THE REPOSITORY -- see the note at the end.
  writeFttDataWindows.C  Daniel's original: writes ONE global entry with a
                         uniform window and no anchors
  readFttDataWindows.C   Daniel's original: prints whatever the DB holds

Use fttDataWindow_db.C for real calibrations. writeFttDataWindows.C does not
read a measurement at all -- it hardcodes min=-65, max=100, anchor=0 for every
VMM and stores a single entry. That is the right thing for establishing a
default window and it is what the DB holds today, but it is not calibration.


Why per-run anchors exist
-------------------------
StFttHitCalibMaker can find each VMM's anchor on the fly, but it does so
separately in every job, from that job's events. On sparse data it never
converges: the zero-field physics-stream runs have about 31x fewer FTT hits per
event than the fwd stream, so the first events of every file are timed against
an anchor that is still moving, and with few hits it may never settle. A
calibrated time cut then throws away good hits.

Measuring the anchors once per run, from enough events, and storing them
removes that warm-up entirely. StFttHitCalibMaker prefers the DB anchor when
the entry is stamped for the run it is reconstructing (PR #24).


The calibrations in data/
-------------------------
  fttDataWindow.23063028.txt            ZERO FIELD 2022, run 23063028,
                                        2022-03-04. zeroFieldAligment_2022.
                                        364 of 373 active VMMs calibrated;
                                        in-window fraction 0.906, much the best
                                        of any run here.
  fttDataWindow.23081*.txt   (43 runs)  2022 FIELD ON, 2022-03-22/23, the fwd
                                        stream test runs. In-window fraction
                                        0.57-0.59 -- the field-on runs are
                                        harder, and this is what the anchors buy
                                        back.
  fttDataWindow.2517[34]*.txt (12 runs) ZERO FIELD 2024, event date 2024-06-22.
                                        zeroFieldAlignment_2024. The same data
                                        the Run 24 sTGC geometry iteration was
                                        measured from.
  fttDataWindow.2022.QA.txt             one summary line per run, appended by
                                        fttDataWindow.C: nOK, noisy, lowStat,
                                        rejected, dead, in-window fractions per
                                        orientation, and whether the fit
                                        converged. Read this before trusting a
                                        run.
  fttDataWindow.2022.header.qa.txt      the column header for that file.

  Not copied here: fttDataWindow.23081053.txt.lowstat, which stopped at 14
  events (status endOfInput) and is not usable, and the png/ and root/ QA
  output, which is large and reproducible from the MuDsts.


File format
-----------
One line per VMM. The first five columns are exactly the DB order:

      uuid  mode  min  max  anchor

  uuid    StFttDb::vmmId(), 0..383
  mode    0 = calibrated time
  anchor  -1 means no usable calibration for that VMM; the consumer falls back
          to its on-the-fly anchor for that VMM only

Columns 6 and beyond are QA and are ignored by fttDataWindow_db.C: status,
nHits, sig, bkgPerBin, sigFrac, orient, sugMin, sugMax. The status codes are
documented in the file's own header lines, which every file carries.


The run stamp, and why it matters
---------------------------------
STAR DB entries carry a begin time only -- the end time is left in the far
future by policy -- so St_db_Maker returns the most recent entry at or before
the time it is asked about. Anchors are NOT slowly varying; they differ run to
run. Without protection, a run with no entry of its own would silently inherit
the previous run's anchors, which is worse than having none.

So fttDataWindow_db.C writes a stamp in the spare slot 384:

      uuid = -1,  mode = 0,  min = run / 10000,  max = run % 10000,
      anchor = stamp format version (1)

vmmId only spans 0..383, so that slot is free. StFttDbMaker compares the stamp
with the run being reconstructed and uses the anchors only on an exact match.
An unstamped entry -- the pre-existing global one, with uuid[384] == 384 -- is
treated as window-only and its anchor column is ignored.


Producing a calibration
-----------------------
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow.C("st_fwd_23081049_raw_5500015.MuDst.root")'
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow.C("run23063028.list")'   # one MuDst/line

Writes fttDataWindow.<run>.txt plus QA plots and histograms. More events is
better: the QA summary shows runs with a few hundred events converging and the
14-event run not.


Pushing to the database
-----------------------
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow_db.C(23063028)'           dry run, one run
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow_db.C(0)'                  dry run, all of data/
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow_db.C(23063028,"writedb")' store one run
    root4star -l -b -q 'fttDataWindow_db.C(0,"writedb")'        store all of data/

Dry run first. It touches nothing and says, per run, whether a push would store
it, skip it, or fail the quality gate, then reads the DB back and reports how
it differs from the file. Options are substrings: "writedb" to store, "force"
to store even when the calibration looks poor. dir defaults to the data/
directory beside this macro, so it works from any working directory; pass dir
explicitly to push a calibration you produced elsewhere.

IT DOES NOT INSERT BLINDLY. The run stamp in slot 384 says whether what the DB
serves for a run is that run's own entry, so before storing, the macro reads it
back and compares:
  - nothing for this run    -> stores it;
  - this run, same numbers  -> says so and moves on, so running over data/
                               twice changes nothing;
  - this run, different     -> refuses unless told to update (below).
Running the whole directory again after a push is therefore safe and cheap.

ENTRIES GO TEN SECONDS BEFORE THE RUN STARTS, not at it. Resolution is "latest
beginTime at or before the requested time" and every event of a run is at or
after the run start, so an entry anywhere in [start-10 s, start] serves the
WHOLE run. An entry after the start would not: the first seconds of the run
would keep resolving to the previous entry.

That is what makes recalibration possible at all. Nothing is ever overwritten
-- (nodeID, elementID, beginTime, flavor, deactive) is the primary key -- so a
corrected calibration has to go in at a different time, and the only times that
still cover the whole run are the ones up to the run start. So:

    run start - 10 s    first calibration for the run
    run start -  9 s    second, with "update"
    ...
    run start           eleventh

Pass "update" with "writedb" and the macro puts the new numbers one second
after the existing entry and tells you where. After eleven it stops and says to
ask the database team to deactivate one instead, rather than going past the run
start and quietly leaving the first seconds of the run on stale anchors.

Ten is not arbitrary: an entry must not land inside the PREVIOUS run. Measured
over 2149 runs of 2022 days 060-099, the smallest gap between one run ending
and the next starting was 26 s, and none was under 10 s.

A failed write stops the whole pass rather than carrying on through the
remaining runs, so a permission or connection problem shows up once instead of
fifty-six times.

Writing needs INSERT permission on Calibrations_ftt, granted per account -- ask
Dmitry Arkhipkin <arkhipkin@bnl.gov>. Reads work without it, so a clean dry run
says nothing about whether you can push.

The beginTime comes from the run's own start time, looked up from RunLog -- not
from Gene's per-run-year slots, which govern the GEOMETRY tables next door in
Geometry/. A time calibration belongs to one run, so its begin time is that
run's start.

NOTE the namespace is Calibrations/ftt, which already exists and already has
the global window entry. Unlike Geometry/ftt it is not waiting on the
stgc -> ftt rename.


What is in data/, and where the rest should live
------------------------------------------------
data/ holds 56 calibrated runs -- 44 from Run 22 and 12 from Run 24 -- plus two
QA summaries, about 29 kB each and 1.6 MB in all. They are committed as the
worked examples: enough to run every mode of fttDataWindow_db.C, to see the
file format against a real file rather than a description, and to reproduce
what was pushed.

They are NOT the eventual home for the full set. There is one calibration per
run by construction, since anchors are not slowly varying, so calibrating every
run of a dataset means thousands of files -- data rather than code, and too
much for a source repository. That set is being produced now from the Run 22
st_physics MuDsts.

Where it should live is not settled. Until it is:

  - fttDataWindow_db.C defaults to ./data beside itself, so the examples work
    with no arguments;
  - pass dir= to point it at wherever a larger set lives;
  - anything here can be regenerated from the MuDsts with fttDataWindow.C, and
    once a run has been pushed the database holds its numbers, so the text file
    is a convenience and not the only copy.
